piranha solution is a mixture of  hydrogen peroxide and concentrated  sulfuric acid it's thought they react  together to make some peroxy sulfuric  acid which is very good as oxidizing  organic compounds  a viewer was really keen to see what  would happen if we put an egg in piranha  solution because we'd already done the  chicken so we've answered the question  which came first the egg or the chicken  it's the chicken  so  i bought a selection of eggs and we  began putting an ordinary egg into the  piranha solution  now of course the shell of an egg  is largely calcium carbonate with not  much organic material so when we put in  an egg into piranha solution nothing  much happened it bubbled slightly and  the solution got really quite hot but  the egg itself remained intact  nothing's happened  and when neal broke it open  it was soft boiled  so this is a way of boiling an egg  but chemically it's not a very sound one  and i wouldn't eat the egg afterwards if  i were you  go on then  i discovered that our local supermarket  sells hard-boiled eggs without their  shell  and we put it in the piranha solution  which we'd already had the egg in so it  wasn't necessarily full strength but  what happened was that the outer layers  of the  white of the egg went brown and there  was more bubbling and interestingly the  egg rotated about once a minute or so  because bubbles built up underneath  and that was enough to turn the egg over  so gently turned round and round getting  a bit browner and still looking a bit  boring but then brady got really excited  at the idea of poaching an egg in  piranha solution  so with fresh piranha solution  neil cracked an egg  into the  piranha solution  and then  we got really quite a good reaction  this is definitely going to blow the top  first of all the egg white frothed up  with the oxygen  and it started to spill over  and then everything quietened down a bit  and then the reaction restarted with  quite an intense brown color which  almost certainly was the formation of  finely divided carbon that we've seen  before  by the end of the  reaction  there was none of the egg left  it may have been finely divided in the  solution but there wasn't anything that  you could pick out  so  as a way of poaching eggs it's totally  unsuccessful  now you may think these are really  rather stupid experiments  but they do demonstrate one really quite  important point about chemical reactions  and that is what is called mass  transport it's not enough for things to  be very reactive  but you have to get them together  mixed properly so that you can get the  reaction to occur with the ordinary egg  the piranha solution just cannot get  through the shell which is largely  calcium carbonate to attack what's  inside with a hard-boiled egg it can get  at the surface but the surface is quite  rigid so the mixing between the egg and  the piranha solution is very bad but  then  if you put in a raw egg which is largely  liquid apart from the yolk  then  you get good mixing between the piranha  solution  and the protein of the egg and bush it  takes off  so you've seen some nice pictures  and you've learned a good piece of  chemical engineering  maximize your mass transport if you want  to get a good reaction  and  really looked almost like an  advertisement for being a vegetarian  really yucky
 

